body:
Thinking about it, the same applies to `Range` requests or progress upload events: if we know that the Angular Service Worker (or Service Workers in general) does not handle to particular request type, IMO the Angular Service Worker should not be trying to handle it in the first place: the support for the `ngsw-bypass` header & query param added in https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/21191 only works in fairly simple applications / demo projects, when one precisely knows which type of request is used where, but is currently impractical to use in real-life large scale projects (as in you can't practically go through all your legacy code & add that header).

My new `ngsw-worker.js` patch has become as below, but I think solving these issues for the community would really drive Service Workers adoption, which are a groundbreaking technology but, due to the fact that they impact all the network requests made from the SW scope (`/` most of the time) - not just the new shinny SPA parts - are really hard to deploy in production at the moment.

```
if (req.method === 'POST' || req.headers.get('range') || req.headers.has('ngsw-bypass') || /[?&]ngsw-bypass(?:[=&]|$)/i.test(requestUrlObj.search)) {
  return;
}
```
